    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Advanced maternal age (AMA; ≥ 35 years in humans) is associated with an increased risk of severe pregnancy complications, including miscarriages and stillbirths. These risks are also elevated in circumstances of infection and elevated inflammatory signaling in the mother. Given that older individuals frequently develop chronic inflammation that worsens with age, it is possible that the immune system may contribute to poor pregnancy outcomes in AMA mothers. This study characterized the immunological profiles of young and aged pregnant mice in order to identify the role of inflammation on AMA pregnancy outcomes. Older pregnant mice (aged 6–8 months) exhibited prolonged and elevated cytokine levels compared to young controls (aged 2–4 months) following mid-gestation challenges of either saline or lipopolysaccharide (LPS), a bacterial mimetic. Analyses of embryonic development from these pregnancies later demonstrated that the percentage of inviable, hemorrhaged or otherwise affected fetuses was significantly increased in litters from AMA dams. This effect tended to be exacerbated following a mild immune challenge by LPS. Finally, embryonic brains from AMA litters tended to exhibit reduced neural stem cell proliferation in the neocortex compared to those from control litters, and placentas from AMA dams tended to display greater degrees of necrosis than placentas from young dams. These findings not only highlight inflammation as a potentially relevant risk factor in AMA pregnancies but also implicate maternal age as a possible confounder in studies on maternal immune activation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
